{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785443136,"nonce":"hBen9eXZuVvLeK5j1ZQwEV34oYA=","title":"The CAS GC's mark walk reads through the tiered handle, so a torn cold tier marks clean and the torn-CAS error never fires","message":"Found while reviewing ADR-0064 s1. Independent of that slice — this is true on `main` today and it is what makes the whole class of \"cold is missing an object\" bug invisible.\n\nThe mark walk reads through the tiered store (`crates/scarab-server/src/retention.rs:188`, wired at `crates/scarab-server/src/main.rs:251`), which falls back to warm and backfills (`crates/scarab-storage/src/tiered.rs`). So a tree or blob that exists **only in warm** is reachable during the walk, marks clean, and the torn-CAS error at `retention.rs:240-251` never fires.\n\nConsequence: cold can be missing objects a snapshot's root references and nothing detects it. The evidence is intact right up until the warm volume is lost or reprovisioned — at which point the loss is total and unattributable. This is the same shape as ADR-0063's log finding (absence is only loud if something looks at the authoritative copy), applied to the CAS.\n\nIt also means the two omission bugs found in ADR-0064 s1 review would have been undetectable in production had they shipped: cold would have held trees with absent children and the sweeper would have reported a clean walk.\n\nWorth deciding rather than assuming: the mark walk reading warm is a real hit-rate win on a walk that touches everything. Options are (a) walk cold only and accept the cost, (b) walk tiered but verify existence in cold for anything the walk marks, (c) a periodic separate cold-integrity sweep that is allowed to be slow.\n\n- [ ] A cold tier missing a reachable object is detected by something, and says which snapshot and which address\n- [ ] Whatever is chosen states its cost, since this walk touches the whole live set","files":null}]}